{"name":"Ganymede","hex":"#8b7d82","fetched":"2026-10-12","looked":{"definitions":[{"term":"Ganymede","whole":true,"parts":[{"part":"Proper noun","definitions":["A Trojan boy who was abducted (either by Zeus or Eos), and ultimately became immortal in order to be Zeus' cupbearer.","A moon of Jupiter."]},{"part":"Noun","definitions":["A servant boy or young waiter, particularly one who serves liquor.","A boy kept for pederastic purposes; a catamite."]}],"link":"https://en.wiktionary.org/wiki/Ganymede"}],"entries":[{"title":"Ganymede (mythology)","blocks":[{"text":"In Greek mythology, Ganymede or Ganymedes is a divine hero whose homeland was Troy. Homer describes Ganymede as the most handsome of mortals and tells the story of how he was abducted by the gods to serve as Zeus's cup-bearer in Olympus. While classical traditions follow this narrative, a different account by Dictys Cretensis instead states that Ganymede was abducted by the Cretans. The Latin form of his name was Catamitus, from which the English word catamite is derived."}],"image":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/3/31/Bust_Ganymede_Louvre_Ma535.jpg/330px-Bust_Ganymede_Louvre_Ma535.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=api&utm_content=thumbnail","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Ganymede_(mythology)","wikidata":"Q131588"},{"title":"Ganymede (moon)","blocks":[{"text":"Ganymede is a natural satellite of Jupiter and is the largest and most massive moon in the Solar System. Like Saturn's largest moon, Titan, it is larger than the planet Mercury, but has somewhat less surface gravity than Mercury, Io, or Earth's Moon due to its lower density compared to the three. Ganymede orbits Jupiter in roughly seven days and is in a 1:2:4 orbital resonance with the moons Europa and Io, respectively."}],"image":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/2/21/Ganymede_-_Perijove_34_Composite.png/330px-Ganymede_-_Perijove_34_Composite.png?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=api&utm_content=thumbnail","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Ganymede_(moon)","wikidata":"Q3169"}],"images":[{"srcset":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/b/b7/Rilievo_con_ganimede%2C_fine_I_sec._dc._01.JPG/500px-Rilievo_con_ganimede%2C_fine_I_sec._dc._01.JPG?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail 1x","src":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/b/b7/Rilievo_con_ganimede%2C_fine_I_sec._dc._01.JPG/500px-Rilievo_con_ganimede%2C_fine_I_sec._dc._01.JPG?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail","alt":"Roman-era relief depicting the eagle of Zeus abducting Ganymede, his Phrygian cap denoting an eastern origin, and a river god","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/File%3ARilievo_con_ganimede%2C_fine_I_sec._dc._01.JPG"},{"srcset":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/4/43/Ganymedes_Zeus_MET_L.1999.10.14.jpg/500px-Ganymedes_Zeus_MET_L.1999.10.14.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail 1x, https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/4/43/Ganymedes_Zeus_MET_L.1999.10.14.jpg/1280px-Ganymedes_Zeus_MET_L.1999.10.14.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail 2x","src":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/4/43/Ganymedes_Zeus_MET_L.1999.10.14.jpg/500px-Ganymedes_Zeus_MET_L.1999.10.14.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail","alt":"Ganymede pouring Zeus a libation (Attic red-figure calyx krater by the Eucharides Painter, c. 490–480 BCE)","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/File%3AGanymedes_Zeus_MET_L.1999.10.14.jpg"},{"srcset":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/8/85/Bronze_lamp_featuring_Zeus_and_Ganymede_from_Vani%2C_Georgia_%28Colchis%29.jpg/500px-Bronze_lamp_featuring_Zeus_and_Ganymede_from_Vani%2C_Georgia_%28Colchis%29.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail 1x, https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/comm